Let the search for the echo's delay learn in the near end's pauses when talk there keeps it from finding a weak echo

With the near end talking from the start of a call (speech with short pauses) over an echo of
speech some 15 to 25 dB under the far end, the matched filters that search for the delay never
match the capture well enough to count (error near 0.7 of the capture, the threshold is 0.2):
the near end's talk, louder than the echo, keeps pulling them off it, and their peak jumps
around. No delay is found, the transparent mode takes the call for a headset after about 6 s,
and the echo goes through at 0 dB in the near end's pauses for as long as both keep talking.
Measured in the AEC3 simulator (M158 with main's patches and 0017 and 0018 of this series, 16
kHz): echo of 200 ms at -15 to -25 dB, near end at two levels, 3 seeds each: in 11 of 18 runs
the echo in the pauses from 8 to 20 s stays within 6 dB of what comes in (median -2.5 dB).
Given the right delay from outside, the same runs reach -5 to -25 dB.

A second search, with its own matched filters and lag aggregator, now runs beside the usual
one until the usual one finds a delay, and its delay is used only while the usual one has
none. Once the usual search finds a delay, its delay is the one used from then on, as without
this, even when it differs or is lost later, and the second search stops. The usual search
itself is left as it was: holding its own filters back delayed by up to 2.8 s a delay it
found on its own with recorded speech at both ends, and its wrong delays were taken up later.

When no delay has been found after 5 s of render and its filters matched the capture fewer
than 50 times by then, the second search adapts only on updates whose capture, against the
loudest render within its reach, is no more than 6 dB above the lowest fifth of that ratio
over the last 2 s: the near end's pauses. Render too weak for the filters to adapt on (their
own threshold per sample, over the 4 loudest sub-blocks in reach) and digital silence at the
capture (a muted microphone) are left out of those ratios. It holds only while the capture of
those 2 s shows pauses (more than 10 dB between its lowest and highest fifths), and not while
the capture's level in dB follows the render's (correlation 0.86 or more at some lag within
reach, checked every 0.5 s): the capture is then echo, not talk. While it holds, a peak within
32 taps of a filter's start or 64 of its end does not count: with recorded speech those gave
delays some 100 ms off.

A delay from the second search starts the echo removal while the near end keeps talking, with a
linear filter that knows nothing yet, and the suppressor would cut the near end's words almost
whole for about half a second. After such a delay, and only on updates where the capture is the
near end's talk (the same ratio, more than 6 dB above its lowest fifth), the suppressor's gain
stays at 1 for half a second, comes down to 0.5 over the next half second, and stays at no less
than 0.5 for 16 s more; in the pauses it is as before. A change of the delay by up to 2 blocks,
as the search gives while it settles, leaves that floor as it is; a larger change, a delay from
the usual search, or its loss end it.
The second search starts its filters again after half a second of a steady delay, as the usual
one does; what they learnt of the near end's talk stays (that they hold back for it, that they
have, the limits at their edges, and whether the capture follows the render), so the floor lasts
as above, and goes only with a full reset of the search. (The simulator's figures below were
measured before this.)

Simulator, same runs: within 6 dB in 4 of 18 runs (median -11 dB). Far end 6 and 16 dB quieter:
3 and 8 of 24 runs instead of 10 and 13, no 1 s window of the echo in a pause 3 dB higher.
Recorded speech at both ends (the far end, and the near end played backwards at another speed),
the near end talking all of 30 s, echo -20 to -35 dB at 120 and 200 ms, 20 seeds: a delay is
found in 147 of 160 runs instead of 110; the echo in the pauses is -13.3 dB on average instead
of -12.0, 271 windows of 1 s lower by more than 3 dB and 2 higher, one of them from -13.3 to
-9.0 dB (the right delay in both, found 7 s sooner here); the near end loses the same on
average (-4.6 dB), 11 windows better by more than 3 dB and 4 worse, words that start after a
pause where the usual search has no delay yet and nothing is removed. Same with the near end
quiet after 24 s and 12 s more of echo alone: one more window from -14.9 to -9.5 dB, where the
usual search takes a wrong delay at 26 s for half a second, as it does without this.
Main against itself with recorded speech changes no window when room noise moves by 0.02 %.
Double talk from the start over quiet music (-20 to -25 dB at 60 to 200 ms, music 12 dB lower
too, 96 runs): 3 runs differ, one window of the echo in a pause from -13 to -4 dB; main
against itself with 0.02 % more room noise changes 83 runs and 6 windows that way.
With an echo of 60 ms at -20 dB in double talk from the start, which main finds late or not at
all in half the seeds, the near end over 6 to 40 s is 0.5 dB louder on average than with main;
in 3 of its 408 windows of 1 s it loses 3.5 to 5.6 dB against main, and 16 windows gain that
much. Over a grid of double talk from the start (speech and music, -10 to -30 dB, 60 and 200
ms, 3 near levels, 2 seeds), windows of the echo in a pause are only lower (18 by more than 3
dB), the near end the same.
Identical: the start of a call with music or speech on speakers or a headset, double talk over
music, double talk where a delay is found by 5 s, an echo shorter than the headroom with 0017,
a headset's leak, a near end that clips the microphone or talks only in the first 2.5 s over an
echo of -25 to -40 dB, with and without 0018's sound to headphones, the unmute series, and an
echo that appears 4 to 7 s into a call with a steady tone at the near end.

Until the usual search finds a delay, the matched filters run twice, but not while the sound
plays to headphones (0018's signal): no echo is to be expected there, and in such a call the
usual search may find none at all. With the signal on, everything above is as without this,
and the second search's delay is used only once its filters have held back, so that one that
starts late, when the sound stops playing to headphones, does not stand in for the usual one.
The suppressor's floor also goes at once and for good when the signal comes on, even with the
second search's delay in use: the delay is then no longer reported as found in the near end's
talk, and that ends the floor as a delay from the usual search does. When the signal goes off,
the second search goes on from where it stopped (if the usual one has still found no delay).
Sound to headphones switching in a call (plug, unplug, a wrong report and a late one, with and
without the signal; 480 runs): with the signal identical; without it 7 runs differ, the echo 4
to 8 dB lower in 3 windows and never more than 3 dB higher.

